  "_id": "6a7f5797757ee51246381afe",
  "lab": "6a7f5796eda1a5feaff0b458",
  "name": "COPPER-SERUM",
  "code": "c",
  "cost": 0,
  "interpretation": "<p>Elevated levels of copper in the blood serum can indicate Wilson's disease, a rare genetic disorder in which the body accumulates too much copper. Elevated copper levels can also be caused by liver disease, some types of cancer, or use of copper-containing supplements or medications. Low levels of copper in the blood serum can indicate a copper deficiency, which can cause anemia, bone abnormalities, and other health problems. Copper deficiency can be caused by a poor diet, malabsorption disorders, or excess zinc intake. Interpretation of copper-serum test results requires analysis of all the test results, along with a person's medical history and other factors. Abnormal copper levels may indicate a copper-related disorder or another underlying medical condition, and further testing may be required to determine the underlying cause.<br></p>",
